                  \\,.
          %p
            In summary, in the language of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"} linear algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representations"} irreducible representations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [V_i]
            constitute a canonical
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} linear basis
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+ring"} representation ring
          %p
            In terms of this language,
            %strong Schur’s lemma
            becomes the following statement:
          %p
            The canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} linear basis
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+ring"} representation ring
            given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representations"} irreducible representations
            is
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                generally: an
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+basis"} ortho-gonal basis
            %li
              %p
                even an
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthonormal+basis"} ortho-normal basis
                if the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+closed+field"} algebraically closed field
          %p
            with respect to the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} decategorified
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
            from
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:DecategorifiedInnterProduct"} (1)
          %h2#GeneralizationsAndVariants Generalizations and variants
          %h3#for_simple_modules For simple modules
          %p
            Part
            %a{:href => "#FirstItem"} (1)
            of Schur’s lemma is essentially
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category-theoretic
            and can be generalized in many ways, for example, by replacing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            by some
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} algebra
            and taking the representations compatible with the action of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %h3#for_simple_objects_in_an_abelian_category For simple objects in an abelian category
          %p
            More generally, part
            %a{:href => "#FirstItem"} (1)
            of Schur’s lemma applies to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+objects"} simple objects
            in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism+ring"} endomorphism ring
            of such a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+object"} simple object
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+ring"} division ring
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "simple+object#in_an_abelian_category"} here
          %p
            For
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#SecondItem"} (2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism+rings"} endomorphism rings
            of all objects in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional
            over an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+closed+field"} algebraically closed field
